00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.000
Now you can see here in the comments
section.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:54.000
We have reference to our end of the
financial year 2020 price targets.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:20:02.000
And what we're doing here is using the
same PE moving forward as 2019.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:10.000
So we're assuming that the market pays
roughly the same multiple for earnings
going forward.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:22.000
So one thing we do to get a price target
is we look at the 2020 EPS number versus
the 2019 EPS number.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:25.000
And we work out the difference between the
two.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:34.000
So the percentage difference, which of
course is earnings growth for 2020 versus
2019.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:40.000
And we work out the earnings growth number
as a multiple, which is 1.8.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:53.000
So all we're doing here in reality is
basically saying that if the stock
actually delivers on 80% earnings growth.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:21:04.000
If we keep the multiple, the PE ratio
constant and valuation constant, then the
stock price is likely to be up around a
similar amount.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.000
So up 80%.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:13.000
So the stock price moves from $76 to $136.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:24.000
And instead of getting carried away here
and calling this a totally realistic
expectation, what we do is, is we call
this our upper bound.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:31.000
So our upper bound price target is
basically a very bullish expectation.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:53.000
Now, as we have this 2020 price target of
$136.80 to be specific as our upper bound,
we can use that number to multiply by the
shares outstanding to get an inferred
market capitalization for the upper bound
at the end of 2020.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:22:00.000
And this comes in at $15.1 billion.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:09.000
And then we can divide that number by the
sales estimate of the market of $1.1
billion.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:17.000
And that gives us an upper bound sales
multiple for the end of 2020 at 13.74.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:24.000
Now, as we know, in this situation,
Zendesk is more of a revenue growth story.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:33.000
So we can utilize sales numbers and sales
multiples to come up with a lower bound
price target.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:55.000
So if we utilize the consensus mean sales
estimate from analysts for 2020, which is
$1.1 billion, we can multiply this by the
sales multiple of 2019, 10.31, keeping
that constant into 2020.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:22.000
And then we can divide by shares
outstanding to get to a lower bound price
target of $102.60 based on our sales
estimates and sales multiples.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:37.000
So what we've done here is we've achieved
an upper bound bullish price target based
on forward looking earnings and constant
valuation.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:48.000
So what we've done here is we've achieved
a lower bound price target based on sales
and sales multiples staying constant.

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:58.000
Now, just as a side note here, as
mentioned earlier, we're not going to
delve into the details of commenting on
gap and non-gap.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:03.000
This is a situational scenario to Zendesk.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:11.000
And this is where discretion,
discretionary interpretation of each
individual situation occurs.

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:21.000
But it should be pretty obvious in this
situation with Zendesk, for example, that
the market is choosing to focus on the
revenue growth story.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:35.000
And the evidence is in the valuation that
the market is willing to pay for the
revenue growth and the maturation or
maturing of a company going from a loss
making operation at the earnings level.
